    "limitation.ge-qrs-underdecoded.affects": "all surface leads (I, II, III, aVR, aVL, aVF, V1–V6)",
    "limitation.ge-qrs-underdecoded.detail": "Measured against the manufacturer's display of the same study: the P/QRS ratio appeared too high by a factor of 1.6–3.1 in every surface lead, the QRS too low (II: 0.207 mV against a normal 0.5–2.0 mV). Neither the display filter nor the calibration explained it.\n\nThe cause previously given here — the escape path of the 4-bit stream — has been refuted on a real export (2026-07-31): the limb leads and V1 use exactly ONE escape per 4096 samples, namely the frame anchor. An error there can have no effect in precisely the leads for which the deficit was reported. Nor is there a slew-rate limit: the longest run of consecutive full-scale deltas is 2 samples in II and 0 in I, III and V1 — a clamping encoder would output the QRS upstroke as a ramp, that is, as a chain of ten to forty.\n\nCross-checked against a LabSystem PRO recording, the gain-independent P/QRS is 0.166 (GE) against 0.125 (Boston) — a factor of 1.33 between two different patients, not 1.6–3.1. That does not refute the finding, because the manufacturer comparison was made on the same study and is the stronger reference; it means that the cause is unknown.\n\nThe scale has since been derived as well: the field in r0.csu carries exactly 10000/N µV per LSB with an integer N, that is, the calibrated number of steps over 10 mV full scale. For II, N = 4200, which gives 2.380952 µV/LSB — read, exact and consistent. So neither technical explanation holds: neither the decoding nor the gain. What remains is either a measurement error in the original manufacturer comparison or a genuinely low-voltage recording; only the manufacturer's system can decide that. Until then: read amplitudes from GE studies with caution. The time axis, the course of the morphology, the P wave and the T wave are unaffected. In addition, the gain table in r0.csu can carry broken entries — in the examined export for aVF — which the reader replaces with the group median and reports.",
    "limitation.ge-qrs-underdecoded.summary": "QRS amplitude possibly too small; cause unknown. P and T waves are unremarkable.",
    "limitation.lspro-footer-missing.affects": "length of the recording",
    "limitation.lspro-footer-missing.detail": "Decoded {decoded} samples per channel. According to the format description, a .rec file closes with a block that states the total count; none was found here. If the file was cut off while being copied, the end of the recording is missing without showing in any other way. How this block is recognised has not yet been checked against any real export.",
    "limitation.lspro-footer-missing.summary": "Completeness cannot be checked: the file ends without an end-of-file block.",
    "limitation.lspro-stream-truncated.affects": "all channels from the break onwards",
